The sparql endpoint has been developed and is located in: https://data.gov.gr/api/v1/graphql

sample query is:
curl --location 'https://data.gov.gr/api/v1/graphql' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--data '{"query":"query {
 allDatasets {
 edges {
 node {
 id
 identifier
 nameEn
 nameEl
 descriptionEn
 descriptionEl
 licenseName
 licenseUrl
 dataStartDate
 dataLastUpdate
 }
 }
 } 
}","variables":{}}'

2025:
The promotion of the open data portal extends across multiple platforms, including the portal itself, LinkedIn, Facebook, the organization's official website (https://vssa.lrv.lt/en/), YouTube, and through live events. For instance, during the event "Free the data - make it work" various topics were discussed, such as:
trends in data management and use in Lithuania;
the data revolution in business and government;
the impact of artificial intelligence;
data in health, education, etc. and how it is transforming the country;
how to liberate and empower data.

Furthermore, the introduction of new datasets is highlighted through social media channels like LinkedIn and Facebook. The target audience for these promotional efforts is diverse and may encompass business entities, governmental organizations, academic institutions, individual users, and more.
2024:
The promotion of the open data portal extends across multiple platforms, including the portal itself, LinkedIn, Facebook, the organization's official website (https://ivpk.lrv.lt/en/), YouTube, and through live events. For instance, during the event "Open Data Opportunities - See the Invisible," various topics were discussed, such as the current state of open data in Lithuania, opportunities offered by the Open Data Portal, the significance of open data in advancing artificial intelligence and other innovations, and insights from market experts regarding data utilization in sectors like health and space.

Furthermore, the introduction of new datasets is highlighted through social media channels like LinkedIn and Facebook. The target audience for these promotional efforts is diverse and may encompass business entities, governmental organizations, academic institutions, individual users, and more.

We monitor and inspect:
- that the content of the opened data fully complies with the description of the data structure (https://atviriduomenys.readthedocs.io/atverimas/struktura.html);
- compleatness of mandatory metadata fields;

Also data prividers are obligated to ensure data acuracy, completeness and freshness. Also, Users' comments on the accuracy and errors of the data must be reviewed and, if justified, adjusted. The implementation of DCAT requirements is ensured by setting mandatory fields, keeping the consistency and completeness of their completion. E.g. a dataset is given the status "open data" only when the required metadata is fully completed and the data is loaded. The portal can filter data according to their preparation status (Inventory, prepared metadata, open data). Automatic data quality assurance algorithms are also developed.

In Hungary, we always monitor the annual results of the Digital Economy and Society Index (hereinafter: DESI) and the Open Data Maturity Report. These two European Union surveys, based on a uniform methodology, always provide a good picture of our current results, our progress compared to the previous year, and our results in international comparison. The Hungarian government monitors these results and takes measures to improve the results of the surveys. This is currently the greatest impact of open data on government challenges in the country. Our commitment to these surveys is also reflected in the National Digitalization Strategy, which is partly based on the DESI survey and its results, as can be seen in the structure of the strategy and the terms used.
One example of the impact of open data on the Hungarian education system is the MTA SZTAKI KOPI Plagiarism Detection Portal, which has been used free of charge by Hungarian higher education institutions for more than a decade to verify the authenticity of papers and theses written by students. The essence of KOPI is that teachers can easily compare students' written work with the texts uploaded to the system, as well as with the content of other open databases and online publications. At some universities, the results of the KOPI plagiarism search must be attached to the thesis. If the degree of similarity between the texts exceeds 10%, the thesis is rejected. (https://kopi.sztaki.hu/)
https://eprints.sztaki.hu/8019/1/i
Answer to the research team's question 1: We have no information. Answer to the research team's question 2: The KOPI Plagiarism Checker application was prepared by the Hungarian Academy of Sciences (MTA) Computer Science and Automation Research Institute (SZTAKI, today: HUN-REN Computer Science and Automation Research Institute) with a research project back in 2001.
The research question was how to create a language-independent plagiarism checker that also works well in Hungarian.
After that:
• 2003: the development of the KOPI Portal began with state support;
• 2004: the public plagiarism checker service was launched in Hungarian and English;
• 2006: many teachers start using the service, more than 1,000 users;
• 2007: developments based on the experiences of the first three years;
• 2009: the number of our users is already more than 10,000, some university faculties are already using the KOPI Plagiarism Checker regularly;
• 2010: MTA SZTAKI started a new research on how to recognize and find translation plagiarism;
• 2011: for the first time in the world, MTA SZTAKI has integrated an algorithm capable of finding translation plagiarism into the KOPI Plagiarism Finder, which searches the entire English Wikipedia.
The entire Wikipedia is used as an open data source during the operation of the KOPI software.
The other main data source for plagiarism searches is the papers and scientific publications that have already been uploaded to the system.
• so far, 248,825 text documents have been uploaded to KOPI by users;
• of these, 73,496 have not had access restricted (i.e. 29.5% open);
• So far, 350,351 comparisons/similarity searches have been initiated with the uploaded documents;
• The system currently has 112,691 users;
• of which 8,427 users have accessed the interface in the last year.

2025:
Yes. We do metadata indexing via SOLR. Results are weighted with title and tags weighing more than description. SOLR also supports faceted search (built in to the UI) and stemming. The search API also allows for complex query filters.
2024:
The 'Advanced Search' feature was extended this year to include keyword search on all datasets and filters such as Publisher, License, THemes and formats.
https://data.gov.ie/advanced_search; 

A general search facility is available on the portal home page which gives easy access to the data.  It provide advanced search features to facilitate more focused searches. https://data.gov.ie/advanced_search; 

The portal uses DCAT-AP metadata; Enabling tagging; 
Allowing sorting by ‘Most Popular’ datasets https://data.gov.ie/dataset?q=&sort=views+desc; 
Allowing users to filter by APIs https://data.gov.ie/dataset?sort=views+desc&q=&api=true; 
Providing ‘Search Tips’ above search bar https://data.gov.ie/dataset              

Main categories of data can be accessed through the large data theme icons which are featured prominantly on the home page.  
A banner to facilitae easy acces to Covid data has been added to the home page.

In general, we work on improving the metadata to make discovering relevant data more easy. Examples of this work would be manually adding tags to harvested datasets- adding further discoverability to data than the publishers have added themselves.

We have 'Featured' datasets those that are kept at the top of the list because they are deemed to be important in some way. This makes discovering them much easier.   We create data collections manually. for example, the Covid banner on the front page. This effectively curates mini-collections by associating datasets together.

The open data principles are embedded in the National Updated eGovernment Strategy 2019-2025 - https://e-gov.bg/wps/wcm/connect/e-gov.bg-18083/1506d64e-0f16-4954-ba0b-dfd77ae58184/%D0%A1%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B8%D1%8F+%D0%B7%D0%B0+%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5+%D0%BD%D0%B0+%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE+%D1%83%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5+2019-2025.pdf?MOD=AJPERES&CVID=nyx-ndJ&useDefaultText=0&useDefaultDesc=0
The Strategy provides for:
- the implementation of a Registry reform, which clearly provides for open data;
- decreasing the use of unstructured data;  
obligatory compliance with the open data principle (data generated by the public sector shall be in an open 
-machine-readable format, allowing for their reuse)

Establish measures for:
o Amendments in the existing legal framework (APIA and relative Ordinance);
o Appoint responsible persons (data officers) from each public body for opening and monitoring the usage of their own data;
o Support public bodies to define their data which to be provided in an open machine readable format;
o Issue guidelines for defining the structure and content of datasets and metadata;
o Motivate public bodies to prioritize datasets and to publish in maximum details high value datasets;
o Develop a channel of communication between public bodies and re-users;
o Increase citizens’ awareness;
o Initiate activities to explore the opinion of the potential users of the open data portal and promote the portal and priority datasets;
o Create a forum/working group with all stakeholders in open data process to engage data community
o Create mechanism for benchmarking of data openness and maturity on national level;
o Conduct a pilot project for cooperation among institutions for measuring the re-use;
o Take action to ensure that consumers are confident about the timeliness of the information by introducing mandatory binding of categories of information in the list within the meaning of Article 15a p.3 of APIA with active links to the place of their publication;
o Develop and validate internal rules / procedures for the maintenance and operation of the Open Data Portal

The updated National eGovernment Strategy ensures the implementation of the current European principles of e-government, introduced at national level, aincluding open data, and builds on the implementation of the objectives after 2018. This Strategy serves as a framework on the basis of which the policy strategies will be created by the relevant administrative bodies and their administrations. The Roadmap for its implementation has also been updated, taking into account the progress made in the implementation of the priorities set so far, as well as the agreements, partnerships and opportunities reached by the administration and its partners for further actions.

1. Many public bodies lack specialized teams or staff fully dedicated to open data. Responsibilities are often assigned in addition to other roles, resulting in inconsistencies, gaps in metadata quality, and unsustainable planning of data publication.
2. Low engagement from local and regional administrations. Although local authorities are formally subject to open data obligations under the Access to Public Information Act, the absence of targeted support, training, and internal motivation leads to low levels of activity beyond central institutions. This limits both the geographic and sectoral coverage of the published datasets.
3. Need to upgrade the National Open Data Portal with a high-value datasets (HVD) section and enhanced interoperability features. While Bulgaria is working to implement Regulation (EU) 2023/138 on high-value datasets, the national portal (data.egov.bg) currently lacks a dedicated section that would:
- visually and functionally distinguish HVDs;
- support automated submission via APIs and compliance with DCAT-AP;
- enable cross-border integration and synchronization with data.europa.eu.

In Bulgaria, several national-level measures have been undertaken, primarily related to the commitments under the Open Government Partnership (OGP) National Action Plan 2022–2024, as well as ongoing efforts to upgrade the national open data portal with a high-value datasets module in line with Regulation (EU) 2023/138.
However there is a lack of a comprehensive strategic approach to systematically address the main challenges.:
-there are no concrete plans to strengthen administrative capacity, especially at the local level;
-there is no national training or support programme for officials responsible for open data;
-there is no coordinated policy for the technical modernisation and automation of data publication.

2025:
A Law was approved and published in August 2021 (Law No. 68/2021) that transposes Directive 1024/2019. URL: https://dre.pt/dre/detalhe/lei/68-2021-170221042. Among several topics, this Law focuses on catalogs and inventories of documents and data available for reuse, including dynamic data, real-time data; with special importance for high-value datasets, (made available using API, with accessible quality metadata). This Law reinforces  formally, the central open data portal (dados.gov) as the national repository for the open datasets from the PA - Public Administration. 
Open data are currently integrated within the National Digital Strategy as single digital unified policy. As such, the following are directly applicable:
1 - Resolution of the Council of Ministers (RCM) No. 207/2024 (https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/detalhe/resolucao-conselho-ministros/207-2024-901536081), that approves the "National Digital Strategy: The key to simplification" (with Action Plan 2025-2026). Particular emphasis on initiatives #8, (Digital Journey for PA), #11 (National Digital Agency), #13 (Data Available for Everyone), #15 (Digital Blueprint); More information available here: https://digital.gov.pt/areas-tematicas/dados/iniciativas-dados 
2 - The Council for Digital in Public Administration, abbreviated as CDAP (https://digital.gov.pt/sobre-nos/governacao-cdap), was established by Council of Ministers Resolution No. 94/2024 of July 25. It is a project group aimed at promoting coordination and decision-making across all government areas to ensure a unified vision regarding technological development and administrative simplification of public services in Portugal, thereby enabling the coordinated implementation of the National Digital Strategy.
3 - Law No. 26/2016, Article 10º |3, Article 19º |6 , that regulates access to administrative and environmental information and the reuse of administrative documents. One of its highlights it’s the implementation of the principle of active dissemination of information, on which the entities subject to this Law should make available a set of updated information on their websites, without the citizen having to ask for it (https://www.pgdlisboa.pt/leis/lei_mostra_articulado.php?nid=2591&tabela=leis&ficha=1);
4 - Commission for Access to Administrative Documents (CADA), an independent administrative entity that works with the Assembly of the Republic and aims to ensure compliance with legal provisions regarding access to administrative information, in particular, Law No. 26/2016;
5 - RCM No. 91/2012, which Approves the National Digital Interoperability Regulation (https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/detalhe/resolucao-conselho-ministros/91-2012-191863);
6 - Decree-Law No. 149/2015, CHAPTER IV, Article 28º |2, that establishes the legal regime for safeguarding intangible cultural heritage (https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/detalhe/decreto-lei/149-2015-69935162);
7 - Decree-Law No. 47/2020. Article 6 |5, which designates entities to ensure the registration and processing of data in the National Register of Animals Used in Circuses (https://www.pgdlisboa.pt/leis/lei_mostra_articulado.php?nid=3308&tabela=leis&ficha=1&pagina=1);
8 - Decree-Law No. 156/2019, CHAPTER III, Article 13 | 3, which regulates the creation and maintenance of a system for the collection, registration, and analysis of data on science and technology (https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/analise-juridica/decreto-lei/156-2019-125560575);
9 - National Geographic Information System (SNIG) https://snig.dgterritorio.gov.pt/saber-mais/legislacao.
2024:
A Law was approved and published in August 2021 (Law No. 68/2021) that transposes Directive 1024/2019. URL: https://dre.pt/dre/detalhe/lei/68-2021-170221042. Among several topics, this Law focuses on catalogs and inventories of documents and data available for reuse, including dynamic data, real-time data; with special importance for high-value datasets, (made available using API, with accessible quality metadata). This Law reinforces  formally, the central open data portal (dados.gov) as the national repository for the open datasets from the PA - Public Administration. Other aspects in line with the national policy for open data: 
1 - Resolution of the Council of Ministers (RCM) No. 30/2020, Measure 12 - "Circular Data Economy", that approves the Action Plan for the Digital Transition,"RCM No. 30 2020";
2 - Law No. 26/2016, Article 10º |3, Article 19º |6 , that regulates access to administrative and environmental information and the reuse of administrative documents. One of its highlights it’s the implementation of the principle of active dissemination of information, on which the entities subject to this Law should make available a set of updated information on their websites, without the citizen having to ask for it; "Law 26_2016";
3 - Commission for Access to Administrative Documents (CADA), an independent administrative entity that works with the Assembly of the Republic and aims to ensure compliance with legal provisions regarding access to administrative information, in particular, Law No. 26/2016;
4 - RCM No. 91/2012, which Approves the National Digital Interoperability Regulation; "RCM No. 91 2012".
5 - Decree-Law No. 149/2015, CHAPTER IV, Article 28º |2, that establishes the legal regime for safeguarding intangible cultural heritage, "Decree-Law No. 149/2015";
6 - Decree-Law No. 47/2020. Article 6 |5, which designates entities to ensure the registration and processing of data in the National Register of Animals Used in Circuses, "Decree-Law No. 47/2020";
7 - Decree-Law No. 156/2019, CHAPTER III, Article 13 | 3, which regulates the creation and maintenance of a system for the collection, registration, and analysis of data on science and technology, "Decree-Law No. 156/2019";
8 - National Geographic Information System (SNIG) https://snig.dgterritorio.gov.pt/saber-mais/legislacao.

