The first and second challenges of last year are already ongoing. One of the significant challenges is ensuring the quality and standardization of data across various public sector entities that hinder the effective use and reuse of open data. Another challenge is Data literacy, many public sector employees and citizens may not have the knowledge or skills necessary to understand and eddectively use open data. And lastly, as in the past year, the lack of qualified human resources
1 - Quality and Standarization;
2 - Data literacy;
3 - Qualified human resources.

To denote the HVDs, we have the filter "Etiquetas" available, using the tag hvd. The result is the following: https://dados.gov.pt/pt/datasets/?tag=hvd
This metadata field is also used to define the HVD category (e.g., https://dados.gov.pt/pt/datasets/?tag=hvd&tag=geoespaciais).
For HVDs, all datasets are published via dados.gov’s API or through harvesting, so the process is done automatically. In the latter case, providers must ensure that the metadata is properly filled in, including both the reference to hvd and the HVD category.
Once ingested, the portal runs an automated process that transforms these metadata fields into DCAT-AP compliant HVD annotations. These are exposed through the catalog endpoint: https://dados.gov.pt/api/1/site/catalog

Here’s an extract of an example of a dataset with DCAT-AP HVD metadata:
<https://dados.gov.pt/datasets/683fc13c57812182a89fb27d/> a dcat:Dataset ;
    dcatap:applicableLegislation <http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg_impl/2023/138/oj> ;
    dcatap:hvdCategory <http://data.europa.eu/bna/c_ac64a52d> ;
....

For the three types of datasets registered in dados.gov - manually, via API, and Harvesting - the following applies:
For manual registration in dados.gov, the uData framework defines the minimum metadata that must be filled in;
Via API, the payload of the request is defined (fully documented in the reference provided in dados.gov), for example for a POST the minimum metadata is, created_at, description, frequency, last_modified, last_update, url, slug, title and uri;
For the harvesting process, which we cannot control, the framework controls a mapping to the provider’s metadata standard.

2025:
AMA implemented a project entitled "Dados Abertos - Definição de modelo de avaliação de impacto" (Open Data- impact assessment model definition; URL https://www.base.gov.pt/Base4/pt/detalhe/?type=contratos&id=9601046) funded under the Recovery and Resilience Plan (Digital Transition). The aim of the project is to:
1) Compile an international benchmark of methodologies, use cases and best practices to assess the impact (economic, social, etc.) of using open data;
2) Define metrics for impact assessment and monitoring.
3) Stimulate cooperation among public administration (PA) entities (central, local and regional) in joint initiatives to make open data available and reused;
4) Develop and communicate metrics and indicators of success for data publication by PA entities
5) Demonstrate the value of open data, with simple reuse cases (e.g. visualisations) of datasets published by entities registered in data.gov, chosen by the potential to generate benefits and innovation, and the relevance of this information;
6) Identify the criteria and model for evaluating the quality of datasets, highlighting good practices in the publication of datasets
This project's impact measurement methodology has already been applied to different case studies in the country. The methodology is based on surveys of data providers and reusers. It assesses the acknowledgement, knowledge and evidence of the impact of open data in four different areas: governmental, economic, social, and environmental. Future steps include increasing the number of case studies and entities participating in the project. More details on this project can be on the file attached to this survey_Relatório Casos de Estudo.pdf
